/* data/learn-c/phy-12-07.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-07.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-07"] = {"7.1":{"s":"Ray (geometrical) optics treats light as travelling in straight lines and explains reflection, refraction and images by mirrors and lenses. It works because the wavelength of light is tiny compared with mirrors, lenses and obstacles. Wave optics treats light as a wave and also explains interference, diffraction and polarisation.","f":[{"x":"n_1*sin(i) = n_2*sin(r)","n":"Snell's law; n_1, n_2 = absolute refractive indices of the two media.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.1, p.158"}],"x":["In ray optics, light travels in straight lines, and the laws of reflection and refraction explain mirrors and lenses. But rays cannot explain some effects: the coloured patterns on a soap bubble or a CD, the spreading of light around a razor edge, or why polaroid sunglasses reduce glare. These need the wave nature of light.","Wave optics treats light as an electromagnetic wave, with a very short wavelength, from about 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red). This chapter uses wavefronts and Huygens' principle to explain reflection and refraction, and then covers three wave effects: interference, diffraction and polarisation.","A good way to remember the three: interference is the adding of waves from two (or a few) sources, diffraction is the spreading of one wave through an opening or past an edge, and polarisation shows that light waves are transverse."],"e":"The rainbow of colours seen on a CD is due to diffraction and interference of light from the closely spaced tracks, about 1.6 micrometres apart, comparable to the wavelength of light."},"7.2":{"s":"Newton's corpuscular theory said light is a stream of tiny particles. It explained straight-line travel, reflection and refraction, but predicted light is faster in a denser medium, which experiment disproved. Huygens' wave theory explained reflection, refraction, and later interference, diffraction and polarisation. Maxwell showed light is an electromagnetic wave. Today light is known to have a dual nature: wave and particle (photons).","w":["Corpuscular theory: light faster in a denser medium. Wave theory: slower. Experiment agrees with the wave theory."],"x":["What is light? Newton thought that it was a stream of tiny particles (the corpuscular theory), which explained straight-line travel and reflection. Huygens and Young, however, found that light behaves like a wave. The wave theory explained interference and diffraction, which the particle theory could not.","A problem for the wave theory was the need for a medium. Sound needs air, so the early scientists proposed an invisible 'ether' to carry light. The Michelson–Morley experiment (1887) found no sign of it. Maxwell then showed that light is an electromagnetic wave, which needs no medium, and which travels in vacuum at c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s.","In the 20th century, the photoelectric effect (Einstein, 1905) showed that light also has particle-like properties: it gives energy in packets called photons. So light has a dual nature. In experiments of interference and diffraction, it shows its wave nature, and in the photoelectric effect and the Compton effect its particle nature.","For this chapter, we use the wave model. The visible spectrum is a small part of the electromagnetic spectrum, from about 400 nm to 700 nm."],"e":"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach the Earth, at 3 × 10⁸ m/s over 1.5 × 10¹¹ m."},"7.3":{"s":"Light is a transverse electromagnetic wave: electric and magnetic fields oscillate at right angles to each other and to the direction of travel. It needs no medium. Its speed in a medium depends on the refractive index. Visible light covers about 400-700 nm, violet shortest and red longest. The refractive index depends on wavelength, which is why white light splits into colours in a prism.","f":[{"x":"n = c/v","n":"Refractive index: speed of light in vacuum over speed in the medium.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.3, p.159"}],"x":["A wave is a disturbance that carries energy. The light wave is a combination of oscillating electric and magnetic fields, at right angles to each other and to the direction of travel, so it is a transverse wave.","The main quantities: wavelength λ (the distance between two points in the same phase), frequency f (the number of oscillations per second), and speed v = fλ. In vacuum, v = c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s, and in a medium v = c/n, where n is the refractive index.","A wavefront is the surface that joins all points with the same phase at a given instant. A point source gives spherical wavefronts. A distant source (such as the Sun) gives nearly plane wavefronts, and a line source gives cylindrical ones. The rays are the lines perpendicular to the wavefronts, which show the direction in which the wave moves.","When light goes from one medium to another, its frequency does not change, as the source decides it. The speed changes to c/n and the wavelength to λ/n. In glass (n = 1.5) the wavelength of 600 nm light becomes 400 nm."],"e":"For light with f = 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz in air, λ = c/f = 3 × 10⁸/5 × 10¹⁴ = 600 nm. In water (n = 1.33) it is 451 nm, but the frequency is still 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz."},"7.4":{"s":"Primary sources make their own light (Sun, flame, bulb); secondary sources only reflect or scatter it (Moon, objects around us). A wavefront is the surface joining points that vibrate in the same phase; rays are perpendicular to it. A point source gives spherical wavefronts, a distant source plane ones. Huygens' principle: every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary wavelets, and the forward envelope of these wavelets is the new wavefront.","w":["Backward wavelets are simply ignored in Huygens' theory; the theory is empirical."],"x":["Huygens' principle is a geometric method to find the position of a wavefront at a later time. It states that every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary wavelets, which spread out in the forward direction with the speed of the wave. The new wavefront, after a time t, is the surface that touches all the wavelets (their envelope), each of radius vt.","Look at a plane wavefront. Each point on it sends out a spherical wavelet, and after a time t the envelope is again a plane, at a distance vt, so the wave goes on in a straight line. For a spherical wavefront, the envelope is a larger sphere.","Why does it not give a backward wave? Huygens' original principle did not explain this, and Fresnel added that the wavelets interfere, and the amplitude of the backward-going part is zero. The principle is a model rather than a full theory, but it works well, and in particular it explains reflection, refraction, interference and diffraction.","The principle needs no medium for light, and it applies to all waves: sound, water waves and light."],"e":"A plane wavefront moving at 3 × 10⁸ m/s: after 1 ns, the wavelets have a radius of 0.3 m, so the new front is 0.3 m ahead."},"7.5":{"s":"Using Huygens' wavelets for a plane wavefront hitting a mirror, the two triangles formed are congruent, which proves the laws of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and the incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in one plane. The same construction explains lateral inversion of the image in a plane mirror.","w":["Reflection does not change the speed, wavelength or frequency of the light."],"x":["Take a plane wavefront that falls at an angle on a plane reflecting surface. As the front reaches the surface point by point, each point starts to send out a wavelet back into the same medium. By the time the last part of the front reaches the surface, the first wavelets have grown to a certain radius.","Drawing the envelope of the wavelets gives the reflected wavefront. Using the geometry of two similar triangles (the incident front touching the surface and the reflected front), the angle of incidence i equals the angle of reflection r. The incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal are in the same plane.","This is the law of reflection, derived from Huygens' principle. The speed and wavelength of the light do not change in reflection (it stays in the same medium), and neither does the frequency.","A plane mirror forms an image that is as far behind the mirror as the object is in front of it. The image is virtual, upright and the same size. You can see this from the way the reflected rays seem to come from a point behind the mirror."],"e":"A ray hitting a mirror at 30° to the normal is reflected at 30° on the other side of the normal, so the angle between the incident and reflected rays is 60°."},"7.6":{"s":"With Huygens' wavelets travelling at different speeds in two media, the refracted wavefront gives Snell's law. Light slows down in a denser medium and bends towards the normal. In going from one medium to another the frequency stays the same, while the speed and wavelength both change in the same ratio.","f":[{"x":"sin(i)/sin(r) = v_1/v_2 = n_2/n_1","n":"Snell's law from Huygens' construction.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.6, p.163"},{"x":"lambda = lambda_0/n","n":"Wavelength in a medium of refractive index n; lambda_0 = wavelength in vacuum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.6, p.164"}],"w":["Frequency (and so colour) does not change on refraction; wavelength and speed do."],"x":["When a wavefront goes into a second medium, where the speed of light is different, its direction changes. Take a plane wavefront at an angle to the boundary, going from medium 1 (speed v₁) to medium 2 (speed v₂). One end of the front enters the second medium first and slows down (or speeds up), while the other end is still moving at v₁, so the front turns.","Huygens' construction in this picture gives sin i/sin r = v₁/v₂ = a constant. Using v = c/n, this becomes Snell's law: n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r. The refracted ray is bent towards the normal when going into a denser medium (a larger n, a lower speed), and away from it in the other direction.","The frequency stays the same, and the wavelength changes in proportion to the speed: λ₂/λ₁ = v₂/v₁ = n₁/n₂. In a denser medium the wavelength is shorter.","When light goes from a denser to a rarer medium, at an angle of incidence larger than the critical angle (sin i_c = n₂/n₁), there is no refracted ray and the light is totally reflected. This is total internal reflection, used in optical fibres and prisms, and it explains the sparkle of diamond (critical angle of about 24.4°)."],"e":"Light enters glass (n = 1.5) from air at 30°. sin r = sin 30°/1.5 = 0.333, so r = 19.5°: the ray bends towards the normal."},"7.7":{"s":"In ordinary (unpolarised) light the electric field vibrates in all directions perpendicular to the ray. A polariser such as a polaroid lets through only the part along its axis, giving plane polarised light at half the intensity. A second polariser (analyser) passes a fraction cos²θ of that. Reflected light is completely polarised at Brewster's angle, when reflected and refracted rays are at 90°. Scattered sunlight is also partly polarised. Only transverse waves can be polarised.","f":[{"x":"I = I_1*cos^2(theta)","n":"Malus' law; theta = angle between the axes of the two polarisers, I_1 = intensity after the first.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.7, p.166"},{"x":"tan(theta_B) = n_2/n_1","n":"Brewster's law; at this angle theta_B + theta_r = 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.7, p.167"}],"w":["Unpolarised light loses half its intensity at the first polariser whatever its orientation.","Sound cannot be polarised: it is a longitudinal wave."],"x":["Polarisation is the process of making the vibrations of a wave confined to a single plane. It is possible only for transverse waves, so it is the evidence that light is a transverse wave. In ordinary (unpolarised) light, the electric field vibrates in all directions at right angles to the beam. In plane-polarised light it vibrates in one plane only.","Methods of polarisation: absorption by a polaroid (a sheet that lets through only the component of the field along its axis), reflection from a surface, refraction through a crystal (double refraction, in calcite), and scattering (the light from the blue sky is partly polarised).","Malus's law: if plane-polarised light of intensity I₀ falls on a polaroid whose axis is at an angle θ to the plane of polarisation, the transmitted intensity is I = I₀ cos²θ. Unpolarised light of intensity I₀ gives I₀/2 after one polaroid. Two crossed polaroids (θ = 90°) block all the light.","Brewster's law: when light falls on a transparent surface at the polarising angle i_p, where tan i_p = n, the reflected light is completely plane polarised (with the vibrations parallel to the surface), and the reflected and refracted rays are at 90° to each other. For glass (n = 1.5), i_p = 56.3°. Uses: polaroid sunglasses, which cut glare, 3D cinema glasses, LCD screens and photography filters."],"e":"Unpolarised light, intensity I₀, goes through a polaroid and then through another at 60° to the first. The intensity after the first is I₀/2, and after the second (I₀/2) cos²60° = I₀/8."},"7.8":{"s":"When two coherent waves (same frequency, constant phase difference) overlap, bright fringes appear where they meet in phase and dark fringes where they meet in opposite phase. In Young's double slit experiment two slits lit from one source act as coherent sources and give equally spaced bright and dark fringes. Lloyd's mirror and Fresnel's biprism are other ways to get coherent sources. A distance d in a medium of index n counts as an optical path nd.","f":[{"x":"Delta l = y*d/D","n":"Path difference at height y on the screen; d = slit separation, D = slits-to-screen distance.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.170"},{"x":"y_n = n*lambda*D/d","n":"Position of the n-th bright fringe; dark fringes at (n - 1/2) lambda D/d.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.170"},{"x":"W = lambda*D/d","n":"Fringe width: same for bright and dark fringes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.170"},{"x":"Delta phi = (2*pi/lambda)*Delta l","n":"Phase difference from path difference.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.171"},{"x":"I = 4*I_0*cos^2(phi/2)","n":"Intensity where the phase difference is phi; I_0 = intensity of each wave.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.171"},{"x":"Delta l = (n - 1)*t","n":"Extra path added by a thin plate of thickness t and index n in front of one slit; the pattern shifts.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.175"}],"w":["Two separate bulbs are not coherent: no steady fringes.","Fringe width rises with λ and D, and falls with d. A thin plate shifts the fringes but does not change their width."],"x":["Interference is the effect of two or more waves meeting at a point. By the principle of superposition, the resultant displacement is the sum of the displacements. Where the waves arrive in phase, they add up (constructive interference), and where they arrive in opposite phase, they cancel (destructive interference).","For a steady pattern, the sources must be coherent: the same frequency and a constant phase difference. In Young's double-slit experiment (1801), one narrow slit lights two close slits, so the waves from them are coherent. Alternating bright and dark fringes are seen on a screen.","For slits a distance d apart and a screen at a distance D, the path difference at a point y from the centre is Δ = yd/D. Bright fringes: Δ = nλ, so y = nλD/d. Dark fringes: Δ = (n + ½)λ. The fringe width is β = λD/d, the same for all fringes. Larger wavelength, larger D or smaller d make wider fringes. With white light, the central fringe is white, with coloured fringes on either side.","Intensity: if each wave has intensity I₀, the maximum is 4I₀ and the minimum is 0. In general I_max/I_min = ((a₁ + a₂)/(a₁ − a₂))². The energy is not lost: it is redistributed from the dark to the bright regions, and the average is 2I₀. Thin films (soap bubbles, oil on water) show coloured bands, due to the interference of light reflected from the two surfaces."],"e":"Slits 0.3 mm apart, screen 1.5 m away, λ = 600 nm: fringe width β = 600 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.5/0.3 × 10⁻³ = 3.0 mm. The 10th bright fringe is 30 mm from the centre."},"7.9":{"s":"Diffraction is the spreading of light into the shadow region at the edges of an obstacle or slit; it is clear only when the slit is about as small as the wavelength. Fraunhofer diffraction uses plane wavefronts (lenses), Fresnel diffraction does not. A single slit gives a broad bright central band with weaker bands on each side; the central band is twice as wide as the others. Young's fringes are equally bright; single slit bands get fainter.","f":[{"x":"a*sin(theta) = n*lambda","n":"Single slit of width a: n-th dark band (minimum).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.9, p.178"},{"x":"a*sin(theta) = (n + 1/2)*lambda","n":"Approximate position of the n-th bright band.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.9, p.178"},{"x":"W_c = 2*lambda*D/a","n":"Width of the central bright band.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.9, p.178"}],"w":["In single slit diffraction a sin θ = nλ gives DARK bands; in Young's experiment path difference nλ gives BRIGHT fringes."],"x":["Diffraction is the bending and spreading of waves when they go through a narrow opening or around the edge of an obstacle. It is noticeable only if the size of the opening or obstacle is comparable to the wavelength. Sound (λ about 1 m) bends easily around a door, but light (λ about 500 nm) shows diffraction only with very small openings.","In single-slit diffraction, light passes through a slit of width a, and a pattern of a bright central band with weaker bands on either side appears on the screen. The minima are at a sinθ = nλ, with n = 1, 2, 3.... The width of the central maximum on a screen at distance D is 2λD/a, which is twice that of the other bands. A narrower slit gives a wider pattern.","The cause is the interference of the wavelets from different points across the slit (Huygens–Fresnel idea). At the first minimum, for each point in the upper half of the slit there is a point in the lower half whose wavelet is exactly out of phase, so they cancel in pairs.","Compare with the double-slit interference: the fringes of the double slit are of equal width and nearly equal brightness, and the single-slit pattern has a broad central maximum and rapidly weakening side bands. Real double-slit patterns combine both: the interference fringes are inside the single-slit diffraction envelope."],"e":"A slit 0.2 mm wide and light of 500 nm on a screen 1 m away: the central bright band is 2 × 500 × 10⁻⁹ × 1/0.2 × 10⁻³ = 5.0 mm wide."},"7.10":{"s":"Resolving power is the ability of an instrument to show two nearby objects as separate. Rayleigh's criterion: two objects are just resolved when the first minimum of one diffraction pattern falls on the central maximum of the other. The smallest resolvable angle or distance is the limit of resolution; resolving power is its reciprocal. A microscope resolves better with shorter wavelength and larger numerical aperture (oil immersion); a telescope with a larger objective.","f":[{"x":"d(theta) = lambda/a","n":"Limit of resolution for two slit-like (linear) objects; a = aperture width.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.10, p.181"},{"x":"R = (n*sin(alpha))/(0.61*lambda)","n":"Resolving power of a microscope for self-luminous points; n sin(alpha) = numerical aperture.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.10, p.182"},{"x":"theta = 1.22*lambda/D","n":"Telescope: smallest resolvable angle; D = diameter of the objective. Resolving power = D/(1.22 lambda).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.10, p.183"}],"w":["Magnification does not improve resolution; only a larger aperture or shorter wavelength does."],"x":["Because of diffraction, the image of a point source in an instrument is not a point but a small bright disc surrounded by faint rings (the Airy pattern). Two close point sources form two such discs, and if the discs overlap too much, we cannot tell that there are two. The resolving power of an instrument is its ability to show two close objects as separate.","For a telescope with an objective of diameter D, the smallest angle that it can resolve is Δθ = 1.22λ/D (the Rayleigh criterion). The resolving power is the reciprocal, D/(1.22λ). It increases with a larger objective, and with shorter wavelength. So astronomers build large telescopes, and space telescopes such as the Hubble (2.4 m mirror) can see very fine details.","For a microscope, the smallest distance that it can resolve between two points is d_min = 1.22λ/(2n sinθ), where n sinθ is the numerical aperture of the objective. The resolving power is 2n sinθ/(1.22λ). It improves with a short wavelength, and with a high refractive index of the medium in front of the lens (oil immersion lenses). Blue or ultraviolet light is used for better resolution, and electron microscopes, with wavelengths of about 0.005 nm, resolve atoms.","The human eye has a pupil about 3 mm wide, giving a limit of about 1.22 × 550 nm/3 mm = 2.2 × 10⁻⁴ rad (about 0.77 minutes of arc)."],"e":"A telescope with a 10 cm objective at 550 nm can resolve 1.22 × 550 × 10⁻⁹/0.10 = 6.7 × 10⁻⁶ rad. Double the diameter and the limit is halved."}};
